@page "/"

<PageTitle>AAuth Sample App</PageTitle>

<h1>AAuth SDK — Sample App</h1>

<div class="alert alert-primary">
    <h5 class="mb-2">Meet Aria 🧭</h5>
    <p class="mb-2">
        <strong>Aria</strong> is the AI travel assistant used throughout these demos. Like any
        agent, Aria has to <em>prove who it is</em> and <em>get your permission</em> before it
        touches anything that belongs to you — and AAuth is the protocol that lets it do so
        safely. Each page below follows Aria across all eight resource servers:
        <strong>Profile</strong> (preferences), <strong>Inbox</strong> (trip confirmations),
        <strong>Calendar</strong> (availability), <strong>Trips</strong> (planning),
        <strong>Wallet</strong> (payments), <strong>Bookings</strong> (reservations),
        <strong>Catalog</strong> (travel options), and <strong>Documents</strong> (briefs).
    </p>
    <p class="mb-2">
        The supporting topology is exhaustive too: <strong>Concierge</strong>, the
        <strong>Agent Provider</strong>, <strong>Person Server</strong>, federated
        <strong>Access Server</strong>, <strong>R3 Access Server</strong>, the authenticated
        <strong>User / Browser</strong>, and the distinct <strong>Original Caller</strong>,
        <strong>Parent</strong>, and <strong>Worker</strong> roles used by SubAgents.
    </p>
    <p class="mb-0 text-muted">
        This <strong>Sample App</strong> is the “golden example”: each page is a small,
        self-contained, copy-paste-ready demonstration of one flow end-to-end with real HTTP
        requests. For a narrated, step-by-step visualisation of the same flows, see the
        <strong>GuidedTour</strong> at <code>:5400</code>.
    </p>
</div>


<div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-md-2 g-4 mt-2">
    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">🔑 HWK — Pseudonymous</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    No enrollment needed. Generate a key and sign requests. The resource
                    sees a stable pseudonymous identity (JWK thumbprint) but cannot link
                    it to a real-world identity.
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-success">2-party</span>
                <span class="badge bg-secondary">sig=hwk</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="pseudonymous" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">🪪 Direct JWKS — Generic Identity</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    A generic Signature Keys example. The AP publishes the agent's public
                    key at a direct JWKS URL, and Profile verifies that discovered key.
                    It is not one of the four AAuth resource access modes.
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-success">agent + resource</span>
                <span class="badge bg-secondary">sig=jwks</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="identified" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">📧 Inbox — Resource-Managed</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Two-party access where the <strong>resource manages authorization
                    itself</strong> via its own consent page — no Person Server. After
                    approval it issues an opaque <code>AAuth-Access</code> token, bound to
                    the agent's signature. The resource is its own authorization server
                    here, like a first-party OAuth deployment.
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-success">2-party</span>
                <span class="badge bg-secondary">AAuth-Access</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="inbox" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">🎫 JWT — Agent Token (Direct Grant)</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Three-party flow with <strong>standing consent</strong>. The Person
                    Server issues an auth token immediately — no user interaction needed.
                    Used when consent was pre-registered.
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">3-party</span>
                <span class="badge bg-secondary">sig=jwt</span>
                <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">autonomous</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="calendar" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">⏳ Deferred — User Consent</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Three-party flow with <strong>deferred consent</strong>. The Person
                    Server returns 202 + an interaction URL. The user approves in a
                    separate tab while the SDK polls for the auth token.
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">3-party</span>
                <span class="badge bg-secondary">sig=jwt</span>
                <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">deferred</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="calendar-deferred" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">🔄 JKT-JWT — Key Rotation</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Pseudonymous access with key delegation. A hardware-backed durable
                    key signs a naming JWT that delegates to an ephemeral key. Supports
                    key rotation without re-enrollment.
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-success">2-party</span>
                <span class="badge bg-secondary">sig=jkt-jwt</span>
                <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">two-key</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="anchored" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">🔗 Call Chain — Multi-Agent</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Multi-hop delegation: Agent&nbsp;A → Concierge → Calendar. Each hop
                    authenticates independently. The PS builds a nested <code>act</code>
                    chain so the final resource sees the full delegation path.
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-danger">multi-hop</span>
                <span class="badge bg-secondary">sig=jwt</span>
                <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">upstream_token</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="call-chain" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">👥 Sub-Agents — Parent-Mediated Workers</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    An orchestrating <strong>parent</strong> spawns a short-lived
                    <strong>worker</strong> sub-agent with its own key (individually auditable
                    and revocable). Three explicit decisions cover the upstream caller, the
                    worker's Wallet scope, and federation; the parent mediates the exchange via
                    <code>subagent_token</code>, and the auth token's <code>act</code> records the chain.
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-danger">sub-agents</span>
                <span class="badge bg-secondary">parent_agent</span>
                <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">live federation</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="sub-agent" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">🛡️ Federated — Four-Party</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Four-party flow with an <strong>Access Server</strong>. The Person
                    Server delegates the access decision to a separate AS, which can
                    require interactive consent (e.g. a Keycloak login) before minting
                    the auth token.
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-dark">4-party</span>
                <span class="badge bg-secondary">sig=jwt</span>
                <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">federated</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="wallet" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">📅 Rich Resource Requests (R3)</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Resource-declared, vocabulary-based authorization. <strong>Bookings</strong>
                    publishes a content-addressed R3 document; its dedicated Access Server
                    grants low-risk operations outright (<code>r3_granted</code>) but makes
                    <strong>confirming</strong> a reservation a <strong>per-call</strong> approval
                    (<code>r3_conditional</code>).
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-dark">4-party</span>
                <span class="badge bg-secondary">sig=jwt</span>
                <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">R3 · OpenAPI</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="bookings" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">🎯 Mission — PS-Governed Agent</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    A human-approved <strong>mission</strong> lets the Person Server govern
                    every step. One run drives all four gates: two resolve
                    <strong>silently</strong> (in-scope token, pre-approved tool), two
                    <strong>prompt</strong> the user (mission creation, an out-of-tool action).
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">3-party</span>
                <span class="badge bg-secondary">sig=jwt</span>
                <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">governance</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="trips" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">🧩 Mission Call Chain — clarification + delegation</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    One mission governs three seams at once: a <strong>clarification chat</strong>
                    on an out-of-mission scope, a <strong>mission-forwarded call chain</strong>
                    (Agent → Concierge → Calendar), and the PS-held <strong>mission log</strong>.
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-danger">multi-hop</span>
                <span class="badge bg-secondary">sig=jwt</span>
                <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">governance</span>
                <span class="badge bg-primary">clarification</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="mission-call-chain" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">✉️ Bookings Events — Availability Notification</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    A sold-out reservation opens up. Aria subscribes through its Agent
                    Provider, then verifies and acknowledges the signed notification
                    without exposing a public webhook. Try a public channel or bind the
                    subscription to your Work bookings account.
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-secondary">aa-subscribe+jwt</span>
                <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">self-jwt delivery</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="events" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">🛡️ Wallet Protocol — Decisions and Recovery</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Aria handles three Wallet edge cases: answering an Access Server
                    question, extending an existing authorization through Concierge,
                    and recovering after a Wallet grant is revoked.
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-dark">4-party</span>
                <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">clarify · chain · revoke</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="wallet-protocol" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">📄 Document Release — Permission Before Consent</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Aria requests a work travel brief. The document owner first decides
                    whether it may be released; only then does the Person Server ask you
                    to authorize the account-bound download.
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">resource decision</span>
                <span class="badge bg-secondary">PS consent</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="documents" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h5 class="card-title">🧭 Travel Catalog — Service-Qualified R3</h5>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Aria compares destinations and experiences behind one gateway. Both
                    services define <code>list</code>, so the grant includes the service
                    name and cannot be reused across catalogs.
                </p>
                <span class="badge bg-dark">4-party</span>
                <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">R3 · OpenAPI Gateway</span>
            </div>
            <div class="card-footer">
                <a href="catalog-gateway" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">Open demo →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<h4 class="mt-4">Prerequisites</h4>
<p>Start all mock servers with a single command:</p>
<pre><code>make demo</code></pre>
<p>
    This starts the Agent Provider (<code>:5301</code>), Person Server
    (<code>:5100</code> with consent required), the eight Aria resource servers
    (Profile <code>:5000</code>, Calendar <code>:5001</code>, Trips
    <code>:5002</code>, Wallet <code>:5003</code>, Inbox <code>:5004</code>,
    Bookings <code>:5005</code>, Catalog <code>:5006</code>, and Documents
    <code>:5007</code>),
    Concierge (<code>:5200</code>), and the two Access Servers (Federated
    <code>:5500</code>, R3 <code>:5501</code>), then opens this app at
    <code>:5240</code> and the GuidedTour at <code>:5400</code>.
</p>
